<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas Arvis [Laws of the Evening Prayer]<br>Halachah 4, Part 2 (§§ 10–17)<br>Based on <span class="lr">LM I:65</span> (<i>"Vayomer Boaz el Rus"</i>)</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 10                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 10</div>

<div class="p">And we are engaged in this tikun of the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, as mentioned above — from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> until <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, which are the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>. For it is impossible to repair in a single day, lest it be in the aspect of excessive light. Therefore one must repair through many tikunim, gradually. [As is brought in the Writings of the Ari"zal: that on account of Adam HaRishon's sin, one must delay and tarry in the tikun of <span class="h">Malchus</span> — which is the tikun of the worlds — from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> until <span class="h">Shemini Atzeres</span>.]</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> until <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. And the essential aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> is on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. And this is the aspect of all the five afflictions that one accepts on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. For then one closes one's eyes from the vision of this world completely and accepts upon oneself all the afflictions. Therefore one merits then to be included in <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>, in the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. For <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>, as is known — the aspect of departure and <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is <span class="h">Olam HaBa</span>. Therefore the <span class="h">Kohen Gadol</span> enters then into the innermost sanctum, the <span class="h">Kodesh Hakodashim</span> — where the essential aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> resides. Therefore on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is the essential perfection of prayer, for one prays five prayers then, which is not found on any other day. For then is the essential aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, through which is the essential perfection of prayer — that one can pray many prayers by making one unity out of the entire prayer, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 11                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 11</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Tefilas Ne'ilah</span> that one prays on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — for then is the essential completion of the tikun that is performed on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. For the essential tikun through the <span class="h">bitul</span> that one merits on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is to bind the entire prayer together, to make one unity out of the entire prayer — that even when standing at the end, at the last word of the prayer, one will still be standing at the first word of the prayer, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Ne'ilah</span> of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. For then is the completion of the tikun, which is the aspect of <span class="h">ne'ilah</span> [locking/closing] — meaning one locks and closes and binds the entire prayer together, meaning one makes one unity out of the entire prayer. This is the essential tikun that one merits on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> through the <span class="h">bitul</span> that one merits then in perfection, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is why at the end of <span class="h">Tefilas Ne'ilah</span> one says: <span class="v">"Shma Yisra'el, Hashem Elokainu, Hashem Echad."</span> Meaning: we are included then in the ultimate oneness where it is entirely one — whether <span class="h">chesed</span>, whether judgment, all is one etc., as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem Hu HaElokim"</span> that one says then — meaning that all is one, whether <span class="h">chesed</span> or judgment, which is the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem Hu HaElokim."</span> This is the essential perfection that one merits through the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above in the Torah mentioned above. And this is the aspect of receiving the Torah that one receives on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, for then the last Tablets were given — meaning the aspect of the Torah that one receives from the light of the shining of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> from the <span class="h">bitul</span> when it is in the aspect of <span class="h">v'shov</span> etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 12                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 12</div>

<div class="p">For the essential <span class="h">bitul</span> is completed in perfection on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. And then one accepts all the afflictions — this is the aspect of closing one's eyes from the vision of this world completely. For even though on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, from the intensity of the judgment that is aroused, one nullifies oneself toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> etc. as mentioned above — nevertheless it is impossible then to complete the tikun in perfection. For it is impossible to merit the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection immediately at the beginning of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, lest it be in the aspect of excessive light. As is explained in the Writings [of the Ari"zal]: if Adam HaRishon had not sinned, he could have completed the tikun immediately in a single day. But now, after the sin, it is impossible to repair in a single day etc., see there.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore even though one nullifies oneself on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> etc. as mentioned above, it is impossible to remain there long in the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, lest it be in the aspect of excessive light etc. Therefore one must immediately draw the tikun drawn through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span> — which is the aspect of the awakening of sleep, in the aspect of <span class="h">v'shov</span>: we return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> and draw the light of the shining and sparkling of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> etc. — which is the aspect of the power of receiving the Torah etc., as mentioned above. But because the tikun has not yet been completed in perfection, one cannot complete the aspect of receiving the Torah — which is the aspect of drawing the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> etc. — in perfection immediately on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, since the tikun has not yet been completed in perfection, as mentioned above. Rather, on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, through the <span class="h">shofar</span>, one draws the aspect of the power of the root of receiving the Torah. But the actual drawing of Torah into the world is not yet completed — only some illumination from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, in order to repair through it some vessels to receive the light. In order that through this we should have further power to ascend in the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, to draw more illuminations from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> each time, as mentioned above. Until we merit the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, where the aspect of receiving the Torah is completed. For on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> the second Tablets were given.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 13                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 13</div>

<div class="p">For this is the general principle, as explained above: all the illuminations and attainments and revelations of G-dliness are drawn only through the light that the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> attain through the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. But this great and awesome light is impossible to receive except through many vessels, as mentioned above. But in truth, the tikun and fashioning of these vessels themselves are also drawn from the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above. For in truth it is entirely one, and the lights and the vessels are all drawn from one place — from the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, where the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> resides. Therefore in truth this matter is among the <span class="mx">"wonders of the Perfect in knowledge"</span> — since it is impossible to draw the light except through vessels, and it is impossible to make vessels except through drawing the light from there. And if so, where is the beginning of working on this tikun?</div>

<div class="p">Therefore in truth, the matter of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is an exceedingly wondrous and hidden matter. As we understood from the words of Rabbainu of blessed memory, the great extent of the awesome greatness of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — for he said that his entire matter and occupation is <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. For on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> the world was created, and the creation of the world is in a manner of wonder upon wonder, on account of the above. For the entire creation was for the purpose of revealing His G-dliness in the world. And it is impossible to draw the light to reveal His G-dliness except through vessels. And it is impossible to make vessels except through drawing the light, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And Hashem foresaw in His wisdom that this tikun — to reveal His G-dliness in the world — could only be accomplished specifically through the lower human beings who possess free will. Therefore the entire world was dependent on Adam HaRishon: had he not sinned with the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil and had he prayed his prayer, he would have repaired everything in perfection, as is explained in the Writings of the Ari"zal. For the essential tikun of fashioning the vessels through which the essential drawing of the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> reveals His G-dliness in the world — this can only be accomplished in perfection specifically through the deeds of lower human beings. And the essential thing is that they possess free will.</div>

<div class="p">For Hashem contracted Himself, as it were, through many contractions — which are the aspect of vessels — in a manner of wonder upon wonder, in order to create the worlds, in order to reveal His G-dliness in the world. But as long as the human being with free will has not been created, no tikun or vessel or contraction has been completed in perfection — for it is still entirely one, as it was before creation. For as long as the contraction has not extended to the point of producing a being with free will in the world — this lower human being — even though through the contraction many worlds and diverse creatures have already been formed, nevertheless everything is still one, as at the beginning. Since they are all compelled in their thinking to conduct themselves according to the will of the Creator, may His Name be blessed, and they have no free will or test to change anything from His will. And if so, everything is still one will and one thought. Therefore the vessels and contractions are not yet considered vessels, since the tikun of the <span class="h">masekala</span> [balance-scale] has not yet been completed — which is the aspect of the test and the free will, which is the aspect of a <span class="h">masekala</span>. For free will is the aspect of a scale — the aspect of a test and choosing, like something hanging on a scale that has the power to incline this way or that way.</div>

<div class="p">And as long as this tikun has not been completed, no vessel has been completed in perfection — since a creature has not yet been created that has the power to act against His will, G-d forbid — which is the human being with free will. For the essential thing is the <span class="h">da'as</span> and the will. For the essential aspect of the vessels and contractions is the aspect of departure and concealment — Hashem, as it were, contracted His light until the aspect of vessels and tikunim came into being, through which to receive the light. And through this tikun of the formation of the vessels through which the light is drawn, through this all the worlds and all the creatures were created. But as long as all the creatures conduct themselves only according to His thought and will alone, it emerges that everything is still as before creation. Therefore they do not yet have the power to receive the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in perfection, since they are not yet considered vessels, for they are all still included and unified in His will as before creation, since they have no free will to deviate from His will. Therefore the essential completion of the tikun of the vessels and of creation was through the human being with free will, for whose sake everything was created. And he is the essential perfection of creation and he sustains the entire creation.</div>

<div class="p">For when there exists a being with free will — meaning one who has permission to act according to his own will and choice, against the will of the Creator, G-d forbid — then specifically the entirety of creation is separated and severed from Him. Since there is already a creation that has the power to act as it wills. And when creation is severed from Him through the free will, as mentioned above, then is the essential completion of the tikun of the vessels. For then the aspect of contraction and vessels is in perfection — for then specifically the contraction is called a contraction, since His light — which is His <span class="h">da'as</span> and wisdom — has been contracted so much that a creation has been formed that has the power of free will, that can act against His will, G-d forbid. And this matter is very understandable to one who has intelligence. For the essential contraction is the contraction of the will and <span class="h">da'as</span>, which is His light.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore in truth the entire creation depends on the human being, as is explained in all the <span class="h">seforim</span>. For all revelation of G-dliness is only through him specifically, since he is a being with free will, through whom the essential completion of the tikun of the vessels is accomplished, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of what is brought in the Writings [of the Ari"zal]: that the essential thing is that all tikunim depend on the tikun of the <span class="h">masekala</span>. For the essential thing is that all tikunim are through the human being with free will, who is the aspect of <span class="h">masekala</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 14                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 14</div>

<div class="p">Therefore immediately when Adam HaRishon was created — who is a being with free will — all the worlds were dependent on him. For the essential tikun is through the being with free will specifically, as mentioned above. For the distancing is the ultimate drawing-close — meaning: because the human being has this power to distance himself from Hashem, G-d forbid, on account of having free will, therefore he has the power to draw himself close to Hashem more than all the worlds and all the angels and <span class="h">serafim</span> etc. For he specifically can fashion vessels to receive His light. Since he has the aspect of such great distancing that he can, by his choice, act according to his own will, against the will of the Creator, may His Name be blessed. Therefore everything was dependent on Adam HaRishon: if he had guarded the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of Hashem not to eat from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil and had prayed his prayer, he would have repaired everything. For the essential tikun is through him specifically.</div>

<div class="p">Meaning: when the human being — with his free will, having the power of choice, which is the aspect of the ultimate contraction beyond which there is no further contraction, as mentioned above — inclines his <span class="h">da'as</span> and will toward Hashem and fulfills His will and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> and attaches himself to Hashem, then he specifically unifies and connects all the worlds to Hashem, draws upon them the light of the shining from the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, and reveals His G-dliness in the world. For he specifically can draw His light through the vessels that are repaired specifically through him — through being a being with free will, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">But <span class="v">"man in glory shall not abide."</span> For he flawed immediately through eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil. And then, not only did he not repair the aspect of the vessels and did not draw the revelation of the light of His G-dliness in the world — but on the contrary, he went back and caused the aspect of the Breaking of Vessels anew. For he ruined all the channels and all the vessels and all the tikunim through his sin. For the essential existence and vitality of all the vessels is through the light that one draws into them through Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. But through sins, the lights depart from the vessels, causing the Breaking of Vessels, G-d forbid. And so it is in every generation: each person, through their sins, G-d forbid, causes the departure of His light — through which it becomes impossible to know of Hashem, G-d forbid.</div>

<div class="p">And on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the tikun of Adam HaRishon's sin and of all the sins of every generation. For on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, on that very day, Adam HaRishon was created and sinned with the Tree of Knowledge. And it is the first day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>, the first day of the year. And then the essential tikun is through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> of the generation, who in the greatness of his righteousness and holiness is able to nullify himself in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> — for he closes his eyes from the vision of this world completely and has no gazing at this world at all. Moreover, he accepts upon himself all the afflictions and all the suffering more bitter than death for the sake of Hashem and for the sake of Israel — until he merits to ascend and gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, at an exceedingly exalted and wondrous level. Until he merits to draw tikunim from there to reveal His G-dliness — until he arouses to <span class="h">teshuvah</span> even those who are very, very far. And the essential wonder of his service is on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. For the essential thing is the beginning — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, which is the first of creation, the first of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. For it is the first day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>. For then his service is exceedingly wondrous and awesome on account of the above. Because one must draw all the tikunim anew. And it is hidden and wondrous where the beginning lies — for it is impossible to draw lights except through vessels, and it is impossible to make vessels except through drawing the lights, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And if Adam HaRishon had not sinned, he would have repaired all this in a single day. But now, after the sin — when he went back and ruined everything, and not only did he not repair but he ruined even further — therefore it is impossible to repair now in a single day, as mentioned above. One must perform the tikun gradually, over several days. And this is the aspect of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> to <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the beginning, which is exceedingly difficult and heavy and wondrous — one must ascend to the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> and return from there immediately, and draw the light gradually and with contraction, only enough to begin repairing some vessel in fineness, so that we can begin to draw the revelation of His light. And afterward, each day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>, one draws the light more and more. Since there is already some aspect of a vessel, one can then nullify oneself more, for there is already a vessel to receive the light. And so each day. And the more one draws the light, the more the vessels are repaired — until in all the nine days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span> from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> to Erev <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, the tikun of all the vessels is completed.</div>

<div class="p">And then specifically, afterward on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, everyone can nullify themselves to accept upon us all the five afflictions — which is the aspect of all of Israel closing their eyes from the vision of this world, and all meriting the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>. And then the revelation of His light is drawn through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above. For there are already vessels to receive the light, since the vessels were repaired from <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> to <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>. Therefore on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> one receives the inner lights, as explained in the Kavanos. Therefore on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is the receiving of the Torah. For on it the second Tablets were given. For the Torah is drawn from there, from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above — all of which is completed in perfection on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore the essential tikun of prayer etc. is then, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 15                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 15</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span> [Binding of Yitzchak], which is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. For on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> we are engaged in mentioning the merit of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span> each time, as we say: <span class="v">"and the Binding of Yitzchak for his seed — today, in mercy, remember"</span> etc. And so we read on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>. For the entire matter of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>. For Yitzchak is the intensity of judgment — the aspect of <span class="v">"the dread of Yitzchak."</span> And according to the intensity of his judgment, G-d forbid, it is impossible for the world to endure. Therefore, due to the intensity of Yitzchak's judgment, he was compelled to nullify himself completely in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>: he needed to ascend upon the altar as an offering, to give up his life for Hashem in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> — until his soul departed, as our Sages of blessed memory said: at the time of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span> his soul departed. This is the aspect of the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection. But nevertheless, Hashem did not desire that Yitzchak should remain there, G-d forbid — meaning that he should depart then, G-d forbid. For Hashem desires the endurance of the world. Therefore immediately when Yitzchak gave up his life for slaughter for the sake of Hashem and nullified himself in the ultimate, as mentioned above — immediately Hashem said to Avraham: <span class="v">"do not stretch out your hand to the lad"</span> etc. <span class="mx">"He brought him up, He brought him down."</span> For it is not His will that one should remain there, as mentioned above — rather, one must immediately return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, as mentioned above. And then one sweetens the judgment — the aspect of the dread of Yitzchak — through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, through the Torah drawn from there, through the joy etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the offering that Hashem prepared for him immediately, to bring up in place of Yitzchak — which is the ram that he offered in his stead. For the offerings are the aspect of the entirety of the Torah, as it is written: <span class="v">"this is the Torah: for a burnt-offering and for a meal-offering"</span> etc. Meaning: through the offering of the ram, through this he drew the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> — through which is the essential sweetening of the dread of Yitzchak, at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Yitzchak yischak</span> [Yitzchak shall laugh]: one must sweeten the dread of Yitzchak through the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above — through which one merits joy, the aspect of laughter and gladness and joy. Through this one receives Torah — which is the aspect of the offering of the ram, as mentioned above — through which is the essential sweetening, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"Avraham raised his eyes and saw, and behold — a ram, afterward, caught in the thicket by its horns."</span> <span class="v">"By its horns"</span> specifically — which is the aspect of the horn of the <span class="h">shofar</span>, the aspect of the <span class="h">shofar</span> of a ram — the ram of Yitzchak. For the sweetening of the ram is itself the aspect of the sweetening of the <span class="h">shofar</span> on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. For the <span class="h">shofar</span> is also the aspect of drawing the power of receiving the Torah to sweeten the judgment at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — which is itself the aspect of the sweetening of the ram offered in place of Yitzchak after the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we seal the <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="h">Zichronos</span> with the merit of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>. As we say: <span class="v">"and the Binding of Yitzchak... remember."</span> For the essential thing on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is the aspect of remembrance. Therefore it is called <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span>, as mentioned above. And the essential drawing of the remembrance is through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> drawn through the <span class="h">shofar</span> etc., as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 16                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 16</div>

<div class="p">Therefore every day, before prayer, one says the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>. For the essential perfection of prayer is through the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, through the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, through which one draws the aspect of remembrance and makes one unity out of the entire prayer — which is the essential perfection of prayer, as mentioned above. Therefore one says the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span> every day before prayer, for the essential tikun of prayer is through this, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And with this one can explain why this <span class="h">parshah</span> is called specifically <span class="v">"the <i>parshah</i> of the <i>Akeidah</i>"</span> [the Binding] — for did he not perform many acts of service for the purpose of slaughtering him? He arranged the wood and also raised him upon the altar. So why isn't the matter called after the arranging or after the raising? But according to the above, it is very well understood. For it is called <span class="h">Akeidah</span> specifically — a term of binding and tying — for this is the essential tikun that is drawn through the merit of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>. Meaning: through this one merits to bind and join the entire prayer together. For through this one draws the power of remembrance, that even when standing at the end of the prayer one will still be standing at the beginning of the prayer. Through this one makes one unity out of the entire prayer. And the entire prayer is bound and tied together — which is the essential tikun that one merits through the <span class="h">bitul</span> in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"he bound [<i>vaya'akod</i>] Yitzchak his son."</span> For <span class="h">akeidah</span> is the aspect of binding hands and feet together. This is the aspect of binding and joining the utterances at the end of prayer with its beginning. For speech is a complete stature [<span class="h">komah sheleimah</span>]. For there is speech that is the aspect of the head — the aspect of <span class="v">"the head of Your word is truth"</span> (Tehilim 119). And there is speech that is the aspect of hands — the aspect of <span class="v">"as Hashem spoke by the hand of Moshe"</span>; the aspect of <span class="v">"forever, Hashem, Your word stands in the heavens"</span> — which is fire and water, the aspect of the right hand and the left hand (as explained elsewhere). And there is speech that is the aspect of feet — the aspect of <span class="v">"a lamp for my feet is Your word"</span>; the aspect of <span class="v">"speak righteousness"</span>; the aspect of <span class="v">"righteousness shall call him to its foot."</span> And there are utterances that are the aspect of the overall structure of the body, which is the essential building of the human being — the aspect of <span class="v">"all my bones shall say"</span> etc. For speech is a complete stature. And the essential perfection of prayer is that everything should be one: even the last utterance of prayer should still be bound to its head. In the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span> — binding the feet upward to the hands that reach up to the head, and then everything is bound together. For this is the essential perfection of prayer: that all the utterances should be bound together — which is the aspect of the <span class="h">Akeidah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                         § 17                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn">§ 17</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"G-d remembered Noach... and the waters subsided"</span> — which we say on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> at the beginning of the <span class="h">Zichronos</span> verses. As we say: <span class="v">"and also Noach, with love You remembered... when You brought the waters of the flood... therefore his remembrance came before You"</span> etc. For then, in the days of Noach, in the generation of the Flood, there was an intensity of judgment in the world from the abundance of their evil deeds — until, due to the great intensity of the judgment that existed then, they needed to nullify themselves completely in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, in the aspect mentioned above. As explained above: when judgments and suffering intensify, G-d forbid, one must nullify oneself in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> etc., as mentioned above. And the greater the judgments and suffering, G-d forbid, the more the <span class="h">bitul</span> must be in perfection.</div>

<div class="p">And due to the great intensity of the judgments in the generation of the Flood, an exceedingly wondrous and awesome <span class="h">bitul</span> was needed to sweeten the intense judgment. And for such a <span class="h">bitul</span>, one needs a great <span class="h">tzadik</span> specifically like Moshe Rabbainu, peace upon him — as explained in the holy Zohar regarding Noach, Avraham and Moshe: that there was no one who gave up his life for the world like Moshe Rabbainu — see there. For Moshe, when he saw the intensity of the judgment when they made the Calf, gave up his life completely and nullified himself in the ultimate, and said: <span class="v">"if You bear their sin... and if not, erase me, please."</span> <span class="v">"And if not, erase me, please"</span> — this is the aspect of the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>: he gave up his life completely. And this is: <span class="v">"erase me, please, from Your book that You have written"</span> — he gave up his life and consented to have his name erased, G-d forbid, from the Torah. This is the aspect of the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>. For when one is nullified and included in His light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, it is above all names and titles. For all names and titles are after the contraction and the formation of the <span class="h">Sefiros</span> — only then can we call Him by names and titles, from which the names of all creatures etc. are drawn, which are their vitality. For the name is the <span class="h">nefesh</span> and the vitality — the aspect of <span class="mx">"a living soul — that is its name,"</span> as explained elsewhere. But in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> in the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, it is above the <span class="h">Sefiros</span>, above the names — the aspect of <span class="mx">"before One, what do you count?"</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore it is impossible to tarry there long in the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, lest one be nullified from existence, G-d forbid. Therefore one must return from there in the aspect of <span class="v">"the <i>Chayos</i> ran and returned."</span> And then one receives the essential vitality from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, from where the essential revelation of His G-dliness derives. For from there — from the aspect of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — from there all the <span class="h">Sefiros</span> are drawn, which are all the names and titles from which all the worlds were created, from the beginning of <span class="h">Atzilus</span> to the end of the world. For everything is drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above, from where the formation of the vessels begins. For in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span> itself, no thought grasps Him at all.</div>

<div class="p">And when there is an intensity of judgment, G-d forbid, then the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who is the aspect of Moshe — who closes his eyes from the vision of this world completely — nullifies himself in the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span>, where it is entirely good. But afterward, when he returns from there, the judgment intensifies even more, as mentioned above. But he sweetens the judgment afterward through the Torah drawn from the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what Moshe said: <span class="v">"if You bear their sin"</span> — very well. Meaning: if I have the power through the <span class="h">bitul</span> to draw afterward a sweetening through the Torah received from the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, very well. <span class="v">"And if not — erase me, please, from Your book"</span> etc. Meaning: if the judgment is so strong that I am unable to sweeten it through the Torah that I constantly receive from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> — from which all the Torah of Moshe and the prophets etc. derives, as mentioned above — then <span class="v">"erase me, please."</span> Meaning: I am willing to nullify myself completely in the light of <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> and not return from there in <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span> as is his constant manner. Rather, I am willing to remain there completely — and this is the aspect of <span class="v">"erase me, please."</span> For there, it is above names.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"erase me, please, from Your book that You have written"</span> — which is the Torah. For there, it is above the Torah, as mentioned above. And from the greatness of Moshe's self-sacrifice at that time — that he was willing to die and depart from this world completely for the sake of Israel — therefore Hashem truly consented and forgave Israel, and the intense judgment was sweetened in his merit — the aspect of <span class="v">"and Hashem relented regarding the evil"</span> etc. And this is the aspect of the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy that were revealed to Moshe then. As it is written there in the <span class="h">parshah</span>: <span class="v">"and He said: I will cause all My goodness to pass before your face, and I will call in the Name of Hashem before you"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"and Hashem descended in a cloud"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"and Hashem passed before his face and called"</span> etc. For all the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy that sweeten all the judgments in the world are all drawn from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> of the <span class="h">bitul</span> mentioned above, through which all judgments are sweetened, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"I will cause all My goodness to pass"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"and Hashem passed before his face"</span> etc. — this is the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that passes before the face of one who merits this, at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>. For then, the shining and sparkling of G-dliness that illuminates and shines upon him from the <span class="h">bitul</span> through the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remains from the <span class="h">bitul</span> — this great and awesome light passes and crosses before his face, in the aspect of <span class="v">"a spirit passed before my face"</span>; in the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem passed before his face"</span> etc. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"I will cause all My goodness to pass before your face"</span> — <span class="v">"all My goodness"</span> specifically. For there, in the aspect of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, it is entirely good, as mentioned above. And afterward, through the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> received from there, one draws upon oneself also the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">bitul</span>, to know that it is entirely good, through the Torah received — through which all judgments and suffering are sweetened, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what is brought in the <span class="h">seforim</span>: that the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy are the aspect of the thirteen hermeneutic principles through which the Torah is expounded. For the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy are the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that passes before one's face at the time of the return from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above — from which the entire Torah is drawn, as mentioned above — the aspect of the thirteen hermeneutic principles through which the Torah is expounded, which are the generality of the Torah. For from there — from the light of the <span class="h">reshimah</span> mentioned above — the entire Torah is drawn, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Moshe Rabbainu, who gave up his life and merited such a <span class="h">bitul</span>, therefore he truly sweetened all the judgments in the world, even at the time of the intense intensification of judgment, may the Merciful One save us. But Noach did not merit such a <span class="h">bitul</span>, and therefore he was unable to nullify the judgment completely — and therefore there was a Flood in the world. Therefore he had to enter the ark, to hide there from the intensity of the judgment and the destroyers, as explained in the holy Zohar.</div>

<div class="p">And the matter is: because Noach did not merit to nullify himself as is proper to the point that he could nullify the judgment from the entire world, therefore he had to enter the ark to merit there the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>. For <span class="mx">"the ark of Noach is <i>Yom Kippur</i>,"</span> as stated in the Tikunim (Tikun 6, folio 22) — and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is <span class="h">bitul</span>, for then one closes one's eyes from the vision of this world, which is the aspect of the fast and the five afflictions of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, as mentioned above. Meaning: Noach did not merit the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> except through entering the ark, where he was hidden and concealed and closed his eyes from the world. For this is the aspect of the concealment that one must conceal and hide oneself during the time of the intensity of judgment, G-d forbid — which is the aspect of the ark, as explained in the holy Zohar.</div>

<div class="p">Meaning: during the time when judgment prevails, one needs <span class="h">bitul</span> as mentioned above. And the <span class="h">bitul</span> is to close one's eyes from the vision of this world. Therefore one must hide and conceal oneself within the house and not go outside, in order not to let one's eyes roam in seeing this world. Rather, one should sit hidden and concealed within one's house, where the place is more conducive to nullifying oneself to Hashem, in order to sweeten the judgment through this. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"go, my people, enter your chambers and close your door behind you; hide for a brief moment until the fury passes."</span></div>

<div class="p">It emerges that the ark of Noach is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>. For Noach did not merit <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection to sweeten the judgment from the entire world completely. But Hashem in His mercy commanded him to enter the ark, to conceal himself there and close his eyes from the world. For the walls of the ark close before the eyes, so that the vision does not scatter outward, in order that one can gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> and be included in <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>. As explained in the Torah mentioned above: for this reason one must close one's eyes when one wants to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, so that the vision does not scatter to the side etc. — see there. And this is the aspect of the greatness of <span class="h">hisbodedus</span> [secluded prayer]: a person must seclude himself within his house and bind himself to Hashem with prayers and supplications, in order to close his eyes from the vision of this world and not let his vision scatter outside. And for this very purpose, the walls of the house serve to conceal oneself within them from the destroyers at the time of the intensity of judgment, may the Merciful One save us — as brought in the holy Zohar. For the walls of the house are walls before the eyes, in order to gaze upon the <span class="h">tachlis</span> where it is entirely good, where all judgments and suffering are nullified from existence.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore when the <span class="h">tzadik</span> of the generation is not so complete that he can, through his <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, nullify the judgment entirely from the entire world, then the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people must conceal themselves within the walls of their houses, in the aspect of <span class="v">"go, my people, enter your chambers"</span> etc. — in order to conceal oneself from the judgment. For the walls of the house are in the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, because they close before the eyes so one does not gaze outward. Therefore the judgment cannot rule there. For the essential nullification of judgments is through the aspect of the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of closing the eyes at the time of the intensity of suffering, may the Merciful One save us, as explained in the Torah mentioned above. And this is the aspect of the ark, as mentioned above. For the perfect <span class="h">tzadik</span> like Moshe Rabbainu can nullify himself toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span> constantly, even without entering the ark. Therefore such a <span class="h">tzadik</span> can truly nullify the judgment entirely from the entire world. But other <span class="h">tzadikim</span> can only nullify themselves when they close themselves up and are enclosed in chambers, in the aspect of <span class="v">"go, my people, enter your chambers."</span> And this is the aspect of Noach's entry into the ark, as mentioned above — because he did not merit the ultimate <span class="h">bitul</span> in perfection, therefore he could not sweeten the judgment from the entire world. And even he himself and his household were only saved through Hashem giving him the counsel to enter the ark, and there specifically he would merit the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>. For he could not come to the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> except through entering the ark, as mentioned above. And through this <span class="h">bitul</span> he merited at least to nullify the judgment from himself and his household, and to sustain the world through this, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that the ark of Noach is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"a <i>tzohar</i> [skylight] you shall make for the ark, and to a cubit you shall finish it from above."</span> Meaning: one should make a <span class="h">tzohar</span> and window above, so that all one's gazing and seeing should be only upward — which is the aspect of gazing toward the supreme <span class="h">tachlis</span>, which is above, above. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"and it was, when Moshe raised his hand"</span> etc. — <span class="mx">"and do the hands of Moshe..."</span> etc. — <span class="mx">"rather, when Israel gaze upward, they are victorious"</span> etc. And similarly regarding the serpent: <span class="mx">"when Israel gaze upward, they are healed."</span> For the essential nullification of judgments and suffering, and the victory in battle against those who oppose holiness, is through gazing upward — which is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span> toward the <span class="h">tachlis</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And through the ark of Noach — which is the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, through which he was concealed from the judgment and the world was sustained and not completely destroyed — through this, Noach merited afterward, when he returned from the <span class="h">bitul</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">ratzo v'shov</span>, to draw Torah from the <span class="h">reshimah</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of what Noach merited after his exit from the ark: to perceive through his wisdom and <span class="h">ruach hakodesh</span> to offer offerings from all the pure animals etc. For this is what he merited — to receive and perceive, in the aspect of attainment of Torah from the <span class="h">reshimah</span> that remained for him from the <span class="h">bitul</span>, at the time of his exit from the ark, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">bitul</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"G-d remembered Noach"</span> — <span class="v">"remembered"</span> specifically, the aspect of remembrance, which is the aspect of the <span class="h">reshimah</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, where the essential sweetening is through the <span class="h">shofar</span>, which is the aspect of remembrance — the aspect of <span class="v">"a remembrance of the <i>teruah</i>"</span> etc., as mentioned above. Therefore we mention this verse on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> at the beginning of the <span class="h">Zichronos</span> verses. For all of this is the entire matter of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

