<div class="ssn">§§ 10–12: Ten Days = Gradual Tikun; Yom Kippur = Bitul in Perfection</div>

<div class="p">Impossible to repair in one day (that would be excessive light). If Adam had not sinned, it could have been done in a single day. Now: gradual tikun over ten days. Each day of the Aseres Y'mai Teshuvah, the light is drawn more and the vessels are repaired more, in a dialectical spiral. On Rosh HaShanah: the root of receiving Torah through shofar, but not yet the full drawing. Over nine days: vessels slowly completed. Then on Yom Kippur: bitul in perfection — five afflictions = closing eyes from this world completely. Five prayers (unique to Yom Kippur) = ultimate perfection of prayer. Kohen Gadol enters the Kodesh Hakodashim = bitul toward the tachlis. The inner lights are received (Kavanos). The second Tablets are given = receiving the Torah that was drawn from the reshimah.</div>

<div class="ssn">§ 11: Ne'ilah = Closing/Binding the Prayer</div>

<div class="p">Ne'ilah = the completion of the Yom Kippur tikun. Ne'ilah [locking] = binding all the prayers together into one unity. "Shma Yisra'el... Echad" at the end of Ne'ilah = inclusion in ultimate oneness. "Hashem Hu HaElokim" = all is one — chesed and judgment alike. The last Tablets = Torah from the reshimah of bitul.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 13–14: Free Will = the Essential Tikun of the Vessels; the Paradox of Creation</div>

<div class="p">The great paradox: lights can only be drawn through vessels, but vessels can only be made through lights. "Where does the beginning lie?" This is why Rosh HaShanah is so wondrous — Rabbainu said "my entire matter is Rosh HaShanah." God foresaw that the tikun could only be accomplished through beings with free will. Before free will exists, all creatures are compelled by His will — the contractions are not yet "vessels" since everything is still unified. Only when a being exists that can act against His will (the masekala = balance-scale of free will) do the contractions become true vessels. Adam HaRishon was supposed to complete this tikun but failed (ate from the Tree of Knowledge), causing a fresh Breaking of Vessels. Now: the true tzadik of each generation repairs on Rosh HaShanah — ascending to the ultimate bitul, drawing tikunim, arousing even the most distant to teshuvah. Over ten days: gradually forming vessels until Yom Kippur when all vessels are complete and the inner lights can be received.</div>

<div class="ssn">§§ 15–16: The Akeidah = Rosh HaShanah; Why It's Called "Binding"</div>

<div class="p">Yitzchak = the intensity of judgment ("dread of Yitzchak"). The Akeidah = ultimate bitul (his soul departed). But Hashem doesn't desire permanent departure — immediately: "do not stretch out your hand" = return in ratzo v'shov. The ram offered in his stead = Torah drawn from the reshimah = sweetening of judgment. The ram's horns = the shofar of Rosh HaShanah. The parshah is called "Akeidah" (binding) because binding is the essential tikun of prayer — binding all utterances from beginning to end into one. Speech = a complete stature (head, hands, feet, bones). The Akeidah binds feet to hands to head = prayer becomes one unity.</div>

<div class="ssn">§ 17: Noach, the Ark, Moshe's "Erase Me," and Hisbodedus</div>

<div class="p">The generation of the Flood: intense judgment requiring powerful bitul. Noach couldn't achieve it on Moshe's level. Moshe at the Calf: "if You bear their sin... and if not, erase me" = ultimate bitul. "Erase me from Your book" = willing to remain above names, above Torah, above the Sefiros, in Ain Sof permanently. From this: the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy = the light of the reshimah that "passes before his face" = the thirteen hermeneutic principles of Torah. Noach needed the ark (= Yom Kippur per the Tikunim) to achieve bitul. The walls = closing the eyes. The tzohar (skylight above) = gazing upward toward the tachlis. This is hisbodedus: secluding oneself indoors, closing eyes from the world. When the tzadik of the generation is not on Moshe's level: "go, my people, enter your chambers." After exiting the ark: Noach drew Torah from the reshimah (offering pure animals). "G-d remembered Noach" = zikaron = Rosh HaShanah.</div>

